{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-arch.md | sed -n '280,345p'\"","aggregated_output":"   280\tproject artifact versions outside its current reference or pinned package\n   281\tcontext.\n   282\t\n   283\t### Full-Fidelity Archive Is Canonical\n   284\t\n   285\t**Choice:** The platform preserves a full-fidelity canonical archive of process\n   286\thistory at low-level entry grain.\n   287\t\n   288\t**Rationale:** Long-horizon context management, chunking, fidelity gradients,\n   289\tretrieval, and bespoke summarization strategies all need the original material.\n   290\tIf the system stores only already-grouped or already-compressed history, later\n   291\tview generation becomes lossy and brittle.\n   292\t\n   293\t**Consequence:** The archive is append-oriented and durable. It is not the same\n   294\tthing as current process state, and it is not automatically the same thing as\n   295\tactive model context.\n   296\t\n   297\t### Canonical Archive Entry Taxonomy\n   298\t\n   299\t**Choice:** The canonical archive stores the following finalized low-level entry\n   300\ttypes:\n   301\t\n   302\t- `user_message`\n   303\t- `model_message`\n   304\t- `reasoning`\n   305\t- `script_emission`\n   306\t- `tool_call`\n   307\t- `tool_result`\n   308\t- `process_event`\n   309\t\n   310\t**Rationale:** These entry types are the smallest useful platform-wide units\n   311\tfor later turn-level querying, tool-call management, summarization, fidelity\n   312\tgradients, and long-horizon context work. They separate the kinds of material\n   313\tthe platform already knows it will want to compress, strip, summarize, or query\n   314\tdifferently without overfitting the archive to provider-specific wire shapes.\n   315\t\n   316\t**Consequence:** The platform does not introduce a separate canonical\n   317\t`model_turn` or `artifact_event` layer at this stage. More specific process\n   318\tsemantics stay in process state, artifact tables, or `process_event` subtypes.\n   319\t\n   320\t### Only Finalized Entries Are Archived\n   321\t\n   322\t**Choice:** Raw streaming deltas and interrupted partial objects are not written\n   323\tto the canonical archive. Only finalized/completed entries are appended.\n   324\t\n   325\t**Rationale:** Live streaming needs current-object upserts for UI binding, but\n   326\tthe durable archive should remain queryable, stable, and semantically useful\n   327\tfor later turn/chunk derivation and long-horizon context management.\n   328\t\n   329\t**Consequence:** The streaming pipeline and browser state model may track\n   330\tin-flight partial objects, but canonical history begins only once an entry has\n   331\tcompleted.\n   332\t\n   333\t### Turns Are Derived, Chunks Are Derived Later\n   334\t\n   335\t**Choice:** `Turn` is the primary grouping derived from archived entries.\n   336\t`Chunk` is a later derived grouping over one or more turns.\n   337\t\n   338\t**Rationale:** A turn is the most legible human and process unit for querying\n   339\thistory. Different processes may later use different chunking and summarization\n   340\tstrategies, so chunking should not be baked into the archive grain.\n   341\t\n   342\t**Consequence:** Archive entries carry the information needed to derive turns.\n   343\tTurn-oriented query helpers can expose user messages, model messages, tool\n   344\tcalls, and other subsets without requiring a second canonical storage layer.\n   345\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
